## Collaboration & Delivery Expectations

### 1) Clarify decisions and trade-offs first

- Always ask questions when there is more than one valid way to solve an issue.
- Even when there is only one clear implementation path, ask for confirmation if the change introduces any meaningful trade-off in the existing system.
- Ask clarifying questions whenever requirements are incomplete or ambiguous.
- Confirm key assumptions at least once before implementation.

### 2) Maintain clean structure and avoid bloated files

- Never degrade maintainability by putting too much unrelated logic into a single file.
- Keep files focused and split code into proper folders by feature/responsibility.
- Create reusable components in separate files only when reuse and maintainability justify it.
- Avoid creating tiny component files (for example, very small 5-line wrappers) unless there is a strong reason.
- Prefer practical, standard architecture over over-fragmentation.

### 3) High-safety flow for large changes and removals

- For large features, discuss the end-to-end approach and edge cases before coding starts.
- If a new feature requires removing/replacing existing code paths, explicitly confirm before deletion.
- For risky or high-impact removals, ask for confirmation twice before proceeding.

### 4) Response style for implementation updates

- After coding tasks, provide concise, clean summaries instead of verbose explanations.
- Summaries should focus on what changed, what was added, and what was removed, in logical/product terms first.
- Use technical/code-level detail only when it improves clarity.

### 5) Summary completeness for large tasks

- Never omit meaningful changes from the final summary.
- For very large implementations, it is acceptable to provide a separate summary file if that is easier to consume.

### 6) Cloudflare R2 signed URL usage

- For Cloudflare R2 uploads and reads, prefer signed URLs in most cases.
- Generate signed URLs from backend APIs/functions, not directly in frontend code.
- Keep and use stored object paths/keys to request signed read URLs from backend helpers.
- For static-ish data rendering (for example prefilled forms, page data, repeated asset views), fetch signed read URLs via backend using the stored object path.
- Default approach: frontend stores/sends object key, backend returns short-lived signed URL when needed.
